A model main effect is significant ($\chi^2 = 31.02$, $p = 3.0 \times 10^{-6}$, $V = 0.102$), with Claude (30.3\%) substantially less vulnerable than DeepSeek (49.7\%). Model identity explains only 1.0\% of total violation variance; the significant interaction ($F(20, 2976) = 1.97$, $p = 0.006$; $\mathrm{df} = (5{-}1) \times (6{-}1) = 20$; 2.9\% of variance; confirmed by logistic regression likelihood ratio test: $\chi^2(20) = 47.20$, $p = 5.5 \times 10^{-4}$) concentrates in specific type--model combinations.

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} highlights three suggestive patterns: (1) authorization scope is the \emph{most model-differentiating type} (52 pp range; Claude 12\% to GPT-5.4 64\%), though this type has the lowest judge agreement ($\kappa = 0.385$; \S\ref{sec:limitations}), so absolute rates carry more uncertainty than for other types; (2) incompleteness is a \emph{consistent vulnerability} (46--62\%, 16 pp range); (3) conditional precedence is the most \emph{model-invariant type} (34--40\%, 6 pp range). The authorization scope ranking (Claude $<$ Qwen3 $<$ GPT-4.1) is stable within the GPT-5.4 judge subset (bootstrap ranking preservation: 99.5\%, $B = 10{,}000$; pairwise orderings $\geq$99.5\%), controlling for judge identity. The 34\,pp within-judge range (GPT-5.4-judged subset) far exceeds measurement noise, but given the low cross-judge $\kappa$, we characterize this pattern as suggestive rather than definitive. Both judges belong to the OpenAI model family; cross-family evaluation (e.g., Claude as judge) would provide independent confirmation. Excluding authorization scope, the residual interaction vanishes ($p = 0.97$; Appendix~\ref{app:c4_sans_auth}), confirming that the C4 interaction concentrates in this single type.

\section{Error Analysis \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:analysis}

The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from LLM-coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes (inter-coder Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$, Cohen's $\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{gwet_2014,cohen_1960}). Using LLMs to code LLM failures introduces circularity risk; however, the mutual information between type (exogenous) and mechanism labels ($I(\text{type}; \text{mechanism}) = 0.41$ bits, Cramér's $V = 0.45$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}) provides structural evidence against this: exogenous category boundaries predict LLM-coded failure modes far beyond chance.

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

The five-model data reveal three mechanistically distinct failure patterns that crosscut the specification/linguistic layer boundary.

\emph{Structural misparse}: the agent constructs an incorrect parse and follows it consistently, paralleling garden-path processing \citep{frazier_rayner_1982,ferreira_good_enough_2002} at a behavioral level. Dominates scopal (5/5 models), conditional precedence (4/5), and coreferential (3/5).

\emph{Information gap-filling}: the agent fills missing information with plausible but incorrect defaults. Dominates incompleteness (3/5) and lexical (3/5).

\emph{Boundary violation}: the agent exceeds permission boundaries when authorization scope is underspecified; 69.2\% involve unauthorized escalation with zero conservative refusals ($p < 10^{-4}$, binomial). Dominates authorization scope exclusively (5/5).

Each type's dominant failure mode is majority-consistent ($\geq$3/5 models), with two achieving unanimity (scopal and authorization scope).

\subsection{Disambiguation Neglect}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity. We code \emph{disambiguation awareness} (acknowledging multiple interpretations, asking clarifying questions, or conditioning on ambiguity) versus \emph{surface adoption} (proceeding with a single interpretation). Overall disambiguation awareness is 4.6\%: Claude (11.0\%) and GPT-4.1 (10.7\%) show the highest rates, while Qwen3 and DeepSeek show zero across all 278 combined violation traces.

We term this the \emph{Disambiguation Neglect}---the absence of disambiguation behavior in default (uninstructed) settings, not an inability to disambiguate. Prior work shows LLMs collapse ambiguity into single interpretations in text settings \citep{liu_ambiguity_2023,hu_ambiguity_2025}; we extend this to consequential agentic contexts where the result is irreversible policy violation rather than suboptimal text. Our mitigation experiment ($-$8.5\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) confirms disambiguation can be elicited.

\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}

Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models), with 13\% universal---a phenomenon we term \emph{systematic nocuity} \citep{berry_kamsties_2004}: all models converge on the same incorrect interpretation, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. Of these, 83.9\% share the same dominant failure mode, confirming convergent---not coincident---violations. Incompleteness has the highest universal rate (24\%) and is disproportionately represented (OR $= 2.61$, $p = 0.019$).

